L1  - https://ankitrchadha.com/papers/wk-drift-procedural-memory-maintenance/wk-drift.pdf
AB  - LLM agents that accumulate procedural memory can reuse successful plans, but what happens when the world changes? Deprecated APIs, revised clinical thresholds, and reorganized knowledge graphs silently invalidate stored procedures, and no existing benchmark evaluates whether a memory system can maintain its procedures under such drift. We introduce WK-Drift, built from three public temporal datasets (Time-Sensitive QA, GitChameleon, DriftMed). It formalizes drift as typed events with a fact-change/procedure-change taxonomy, defines a four-phase protocol (learn, drift, evaluate, recover), and introduces entity-plan linkage for targeted maintenance; because drift events are supplied, it isolates maintenance from drift detection. Across ten configurations spanning no-memory, unstructured (including Mem0 and MemRL), tagged, and graph-based memory, graph-based surgical updates reach 90% post-drift accuracy on knowledge QA (+6pp over stale baselines), 64% on clinical (+26.5pp), and 57% on code (+7.4pp), cutting staleness from 77–86% to 41–42%. The advantage concentrates on procedure-change drift (+17.4pp) and scales with propagation depth (+50pp at depth 3); on code, structured retrieval rather than maintenance is the dominant benefit. Ablations split the maintenance effect evenly between invalidating stale plans and supplying the replacement value, and the same corrected content is worth only 2.5pp when it is not linked to the plans it invalidates: what matters is applying the correction, not possessing it. Surgical maintenance reaches 59% in the first post-drift epoch against 26% for stale baselines, eliminating multi-epoch recovery latency.
